# The message log and its badge.
#
# Every diagnostic goban produced used to go to last_run.log and nowhere else,
# so "my engine does not work" was indistinguishable from "the application does
# nothing". This asserts the other half of that fix: that something the user can
# see actually appears, and that opening the panel is what clears it.
#
# log_badge is the state key that matters — it is what is visible *without*
# opening anything. Asserting log_count alone would pass even if the badge never
# showed, which is precisely the bug this feature exists to prevent.

# A healthy startup is silent. The sink takes warnings and errors only, so
# everything that goes right — engine names, coach assignment, the SGF that was
# loaded, and every GTP command — stays in last_run.log and out of the user's
# way. A badge that is always up is a badge nobody reads.
expect log_count 0
expect log_badge none
expect log_open false

# The panel opens and closes on command. Closing matters more than it looks:
# opening marks the messages seen, which empties the badge, which used to hide
# the status line — leaving the panel up with nothing to click but a menu entry
# nobody thinks to look in. The line now shows a close header while open.
toggle_log
expect log_open true

toggle_log
expect log_open false

# A real failure, through the ordinary code path: no such file, so GameRecord
# logs an error. Nothing here calls the log directly — the point is that an
# existing spdlog::error call site surfaces because the sink is installed.
load_sgf tests/data/does_not_exist.sgf
settle

expect log_badge error
expect log_count >= 1

# Opening the panel is what "seen" means. The entries stay; only the badge goes.
toggle_log on
expect log_open true
expect log_badge none
expect log_count >= 1

toggle_log off
expect log_badge none

# Clearing empties the buffer, and a later message flags again.
log_clear
expect log_count 0
expect log_badge none

load_sgf tests/data/still_not_there.sgf
settle
expect log_badge error
expect log_count >= 1

# Closing is still possible after the badge has been cleared — the dead end
# above. Open with messages pending, which marks them seen, then close.
toggle_log on
expect log_open true
expect log_badge none
expect log_unseen 0
toggle_log
expect log_open false

# And clearing from inside the panel does not strand it open either.
load_sgf tests/data/gone_again.sgf
settle
toggle_log on
log_clear
expect log_count 0
expect log_open true
toggle_log
expect log_open false

# The badge counts what arrived since the last look, not the buffer.
log_clear
expect log_unseen 0
load_sgf tests/data/nope_one.sgf
settle
load_sgf tests/data/nope_two.sgf
settle
# Two failed loads, each logging a warning and an error.
expect log_unseen >= 2
expect log_badge error
toggle_log on
expect log_unseen 0
toggle_log
expect log_badge none
